  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 244 PM EDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nWednesday Evening - Thursday Night...\n\nAs the aforementioned strong system moves through the upper Midwest \nand Great Lakes, this will bring a cold front through the Ohio \nValley. Along and ahead of this cold front, widespread showers and \nstorms are expected. Troughing associated with this system will \nbrush the northern half of the region. This will place better shear \nvalues (35-40kts) over the northern half of the region. Southerly \nflow will continue to pump moisture into the region as dew points \nwill be in the low-mid 70s Wednesday and Thursday. PWATs will \nincrease to about 2.0 inches ahead of the cold front. A strong LLJ \nlooks to set up right along the front, which may lead to some \nconvective training over the northern half of the region. As of this \nforecast, there is a chance for Marginal severe storms and flooding. \nLooking to see a widespread 0.25-0.5 inches of rain, with areas in \nsouthern Indiana and northern Kentucky seeing up to 1 inch. The \nlimiting factor for severe storms will be instability, depending on \nthe timing of this front. Currently, the cold front is expected to \napproach the region Wednesday evening and slowly push south through \nThursday as troughing aloft weakens. \n\nFriday - Sunday...\n\nHigh pressure will follow the cold front and help to bring \ntemperatures back into the mid 80s. Chances for isolated to \nscattered showers and storms will remain over the southern half of \nthe region into the start of the weekend. These chances will \nsteadily creep north through the weekend as returning southerly flow \nincreases temperatures and moisture. There is weak overall flow, so \ndetails will become clearer over the next few forecast cycles.",
